verify.adapter_contract_corrections:
Corrections and additions to the study's adapter_contract. All verified at c7dc78a.

(1) Rule 1: the API key is effectively host root, not just the Docker socket. Any create caller can do all of the following, with no operator gate:
- request extensions bootstrap.execd.isolation=enable, which gives SYS_ADMIN, apparmor/seccomp unconfined, and MaskedPaths/ReadonlyPaths cleared (docker_service.py:899-922; container_ops.py:422-424);
- request platform.os=windows, which removes NET_ADMIN/NET_RAW from cap_drop and adds them to cap_add even in sidecar-netns policy mode, attaches /dev/kvm and /dev/net/tun, and drops mem/cpu limits (windows_profile.py:41-42,90-116; docker_service.py:767-769,888-891);
- mount any existing Docker named volume, including another sandbox's opensandbox-runtime-<id> (volumes.py:152-199);
- read any sandbox's egress token through GET /endpoints/18080 (networking.py:340-353).
AISEF must hold the key only in the controller, never request windows or isolation, and never use the pvc backend.

(2) Rule 2 needs more items:
- Run the workload as a non-root image USER that is not 10042, or add SETUID/SETGID to drop_capabilities. The mitmproxy uid 10042 is exempt from the transparent-MITM redirect and gets an nft accept to the upstream proxy, and both apply to the whole shared netns (transparent.go:42,68; upstream.go:67-68).
- Add explicit IP deny rules for the sidecar's resolv.conf nameservers, or point the Docker daemon DNS at a controlled resolver that serves no DoH. Those IPs are otherwise allowed on every port (nameserver.go:26-46,72-91; nft.go:55-56). The DNS proxy's own upstream queries carry mark 0x1 and are accepted earlier (manager.go:259), so the deny rules do not break resolution.
- Never set OPENSANDBOX_EGRESS_POLICY_FILE, or never point it under /opt/opensandbox. It can be set from request env and overrides the env rules on restart (constants.py:100; persist.go:60-87).
- DoH-443 blocking cannot be enabled through the server's request env (constants.py:89-101). It needs a custom egress image ENV, and an empty blocklist drops all of port 443.
- Always pass a timeout; otherwise the sandbox gets a manual-cleanup label (container_ops.py:292-295).
- No server-side default limits exist, and the egress sidecar itself gets no cap_drop, no-new-privileges, pids or memory limits. It runs as root with NET_ADMIN, and its image ships sudo, nmap, tcpdump, strace, netcat and curl (networking.py:510-524; egress Dockerfile:81-104). Treat it as an unhardened trusted component.

(3) Rule 3 understates execd's /internal/init. Unauthenticated, it can kill user processes, replace envs, and run arbitrary preStart and periodic hook commands (init_runtime.go:251-273; lifecycle/runner.go:64-79). Reachability of port 44772 is therefore the entire boundary. Also, docs/architecture/data-plane/execd.md:31 is wrong when it says only liveness and readiness are open.

(4) Rule 7 needs more probes:
- TCP 443/80 to each nameserver IP in the sidecar's /etc/resolv.conf (for example 8.8.8.8:443 DoH, an exfiltration channel);
- a connection to an allowlisted provider's resolved IP with a non-allowlisted SNI/Host, since CDN IP sharing makes the allowlist IP-granular (manager.go:291-294, DNS-learned dynamic sets);
- a setuid(10042) attempt followed by egress, if the workload can be root;
- IPv6 egress, because the sidecar silently retries without the disable_ipv6 sysctls if the daemon rejects them (networking.py:560-575);
- :18080 /policy and /credential-vault without a token, and the host-published 18080;
- 'DoH to 1.1.1.1:443' as a non-resolver target.
In dns+nft, DoT on 853 is always dropped (nft.go:112).

(5) Rule 8, standalone sidecar: set OPENSANDBOX_EGRESS_MODE exactly to 'dns+nft' and verify it took effect, for example from the 'enforcement mode' log line or `nft list table inet opensandbox`. The silent dns-only fallback (main.go:223-230) only bites in standalone use, because the OSB server validates the mode as a Literal (config.py:1001-1004). Also pin base images: the execd build uses alpine:latest (components/execd/Dockerfile:77, not the egress Dockerfile).

(6) Kubernetes: if AISEF ever uses it, also drop NET_RAW. OSB drops only NET_ADMIN on the main container (egress_helper.py:66-83), and CAP_NET_RAW allows SO_MARK on kernels 5.17 and later (INFERENCE).

(7) The compose example cannot be used as-is: it would abort at startup without OPENSANDBOX_INSECURE_SERVER=YES (startup_guard.py:112-117), and the server image runs as root.
